Models
------
Qwen3-ASR-0.6B, Copyright Alibaba Cloud (Qwen team), Apache License 2.0.
  The adapter reads the latents of its audio encoder.
Laya and Laya multilingual, Apache License 2.0. Built on ModernBERT-large
  (Answer.AI / LightOn, Apache License 2.0) and mmBERT-base (JHU CLSP, MIT).
NVIDIA parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3, Copyright NVIDIA, CC BY 4.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Its transcripts and word
  timings were used as training labels.

Training data
-------------
LibriTTS-R: Y. Koizumi et al., "LibriTTS-R: A Restored Multi-Speaker
  Text-to-Speech Corpus", 2023. CC BY 4.0. https://www.openslr.org/141/
  Derived from LibriTTS (H. Zen et al., CC BY 4.0) and LibriVox recordings
  (public domain).

VoxPopuli: C. Wang et al., "VoxPopuli: A Large-Scale Multilingual Speech
  Corpus for Representation Learning, Semi-Supervised Learning and
  Interpretation", ACL 2021. Transcriptions CC0.
  https://github.com/facebookresearch/voxpopuli
  Source of the audio: European Parliament, (c) European Union, 2017-2018.
  Reproduction authorised, provided the source is acknowledged.

AMI Meeting Corpus: J. Carletta et al., "The AMI Meeting Corpus", 2005.
  Copyright the AMI Consortium (University of Edinburgh and partners).
  CC BY 4.0. https://groups.inf.ed.ac.uk/ami/corpus/

Multilingual Spoken Words Corpus: M. Mazumder et al., "Multilingual Spoken
  Words Corpus", NeurIPS 2021 Datasets and Benchmarks. MLCommons, CC BY 4.0.
  https://mlcommons.org/datasets/multilingual-spoken-words/
  Derived from Mozilla Common Voice (CC0).

Timers and Such v1.0: L. Lugosch, P. Papreja, M. Ravanelli, A. Heba,
  T. Parcollet, "Timers and Such: A Practical Benchmark for Spoken Language
  Understanding with Numbers", NeurIPS 2021 Datasets and Benchmarks.
  CC0 1.0 (credited, not required). Only the real recordings were used.
  https://zenodo.org/records/4623772

OpenSLR 28 (RIRS_NOISES): T. Ko et al., "A Study on Data Augmentation of
  Reverberant Speech for Robust Speech Recognition", ICASSP 2017.
  Simulated room impulse responses, Apache License 2.0; point-source noises,
  public domain (Freesound). https://www.openslr.org/28/

MUSAN: D. Snyder, G. Chen, D. Povey, "MUSAN: A Music, Speech, and Noise
  Corpus", 2015. https://www.openslr.org/17/
  Only recordings licensed CC BY (3.0 or 4.0) or public domain were used.
  Each one is credited, with title, author, licence and source URL, in
  ATTRIBUTIONS.tsv, which ships with this NOTICE. Among the authors credited
  there: Kevin MacLeod (incompetech.com, CC BY 3.0) and the SoundBible.com
  recorders (CC BY 3.0).
